replies.text:
@HiddenCanada Glad to be of help.  The HSC has a lot of renovation history to unravel.  There were so many changes in the years I was there (1991-2004)  that I'm left wondering how future generations would puzzle over some of the design decisions.  Building code changes were relaxed when we sprinklered all of the old buildings, so several staircases were deleted and converted to storage spaces.  The stairwell penthouses remained though, so now there's rooms on the roof with no access but by the roof via one of the surviving stairs.  Then there's remnants of obsolete technology like the incinerator system built into the stairs of the old Women's Hospital.  1950s tech that was too dangerous to maintain, so they bolted the hatches closed.  You may still be able to find the old security clock punch stations.  Little metal boxes with a key on a chain inside.  If you don't know what it's for it's a head-scratcher.  

The very top of the dome at the Legislature has graffiti left by visitors, including past politicians.  I seem to recall there was a New Year's Eve custom of climbing all the way up to write something if you were new to the assembly.  It's a series of wrought-iron spiral staircases to get there, then a gangway to the last hatch under the Golden Boy.  They are original to the building and sway a lot, quite sketchy.

The Manitoba Archives Building is a fascinating labyrinth with a building-inside-a-building for the HBC archives.  It used to be an auditorium that was converted to its present use.  The public only ever sees the entry and library wings on either side, but the centre is a confusion of layers of archival volumes.
